#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286" :question/problem "The path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0)$, and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/962f5b7322eaec11b61a09faa7e19175.png)\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2$." :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}(t) &= b + t(c - b) \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0⟩ + t(⟨1, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n&= ⟨t, t⟩ \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1$. Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) &= (2x - 3y)i + (2y - 5x)j \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t)i + (2t - 5t)j \\\\\n&=-ti - 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t) &= (-ti - 3tj) \\cdot (i + j) \\\\\n&=-t \\cdot 1 + (-3t) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-4t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}F(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}(-4t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2t^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2 + 0] \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319" :question/problem "Consider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2$, and $C_3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/4c07015f012adfb9c576718541a44fe8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C$, which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(-C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(-C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= - ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2$. Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y$." :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nP = 3x+2\n$$\n\nand $Q = -y^3$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C)(3x + 2)dx - y^{3}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a436c1fe-9875-5164-ae87-aa8cc5576ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177695} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cf854b-fd8f-54e9-82b6-a3ed3d261ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177696} {:answer/id #uuid "075dc6a4-8af8-5cf3-ae6f-ca3c20cc6c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)x^{2}dx - y^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177697} {:answer/id #uuid "46551c98-a762-5158-93fb-2a0daa1f08f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177698} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3538cb-9aa2-5a37-a8c8-64931e534447" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)(y^{2} + x^{2} + 5)dxdydz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2cf854b-fd8f-54e9-82b6-a3ed3d261ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177696} :answer-field/id #uuid "e716d21b-fca4-5aab-a10e-a4a0863ac589" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177694}] :question/id #uuid "287c4c83-1be4-5535-a78c-1deaf55cde76" :question/math-academy-id "q-125370" :question/problem "Let $F(x, y, z) = y^{2}i - x^{2}j + 5k$ and let $C$ be a path in $R^{3}$. Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8926a693-1f7e-567d-a227-1b4fa9e92923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178530} {:answer/id #uuid "d24f1213-97fc-58c4-a966-fccb5d6dcdec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178531} {:answer/id #uuid "0613f2d8-f1ae-5a9d-ba57-9fc9262ff1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178532} {:answer/id #uuid "31292df5-b34d-5769-a65d-5343211b9c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178533} {:answer/id #uuid "60387dd7-0cc8-5601-a6e6-9e5939d3c07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31292df5-b34d-5769-a65d-5343211b9c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178533} :answer-field/id #uuid "23ee968d-e67d-5109-b10b-5cc8ac6187ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178529}] :question/id #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39342" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39342.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 0)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 0)$ to the point $B(1, 1)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + (x - y)j$, and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d784816-14e3-5c0d-be28-8d2e041f355a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178542} {:answer/id #uuid "c1da1434-cc23-5789-a91f-6a2625bc25cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178543} {:answer/id #uuid "04e4bb00-e83e-5185-915e-cda7617c7335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178544} {:answer/id #uuid "438d5d79-fb9c-5c0a-9f6d-01201cea0e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178545} {:answer/id #uuid "70a7766d-d168-54e8-9ee8-b8df185dee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a7766d-d168-54e8-9ee8-b8df185dee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178546} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde3ad9e-b9ef-522c-a7a2-d1a50bb43f20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178541}] :question/id #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-39674" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39674.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y = x^{2}$ from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 4)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 4)$ to the point $B(0, 4)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr =-2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = xi + yj$ and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec9ccec-70d9-5a10-bb9c-5c852d4244e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179160} {:answer/id #uuid "d485301e-1b2e-5cd1-ace2-fa2cb910d4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179161} {:answer/id #uuid "34cfd759-688b-5922-9939-ca560d18e574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179162} {:answer/id #uuid "385b75bd-9c80-5524-85ff-5ec3f2305a36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179163} {:answer/id #uuid "4d262d15-f06f-58f4-9da8-f151b31224ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ec9ccec-70d9-5a10-bb9c-5c852d4244e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179160} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9856724-1130-5fdf-8068-1d852dd69dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179159}] :question/id #uuid "9aa3be4f-70c9-57d5-8b81-21b143de9479" :question/math-academy-id "q-76473" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-76473.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO$, shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"}]}
